Z||000000||Htown86GT||04-06-2001||01:24 PM||||Has anyone ever drilled out a broken exhaust manifold bolt with out dropping the engine? I have one on the front and one on the back that is broken....Uggghhh   <P>------------------<BR>86' GT  17inch Cobras Sus-tec lowered<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.rav-ee.com/images/Myfiero.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Htown86GT||04-06-2001||01:26 PM||||OOps wrong form....help anyway?? =)||||reg||6
Z||000002||c m west||04-06-2001||01:30 PM||||access to the rear of the block is possible with a drill motor and an easy out - however as for as the front side, remove the exhaust, starter, and A/C as well as all brackets and you might be able to get at it - if not - at least you alreay removed the components to drop the motor anyways.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mark||04-06-2001||04:52 PM||||I was able to easily remove a broken exhaust manifold bolt on the rear head using an easy-out and a right angle drill (I doubt that it would have been possible without the right-angle drill).  <P>As for the front head, I've not had this (dis)pleasure, but removing the exhaust system from the wye-pipe joint on back would certainly help alot.  Also, you may be able to get lots more access by unbolting the front cradle bolts, then slightly tilting the front of the cradle downward.  I believe this is the method that GM recommended during the recall changes to the front manifold.||||reg||1
Z||000004||sjp777||04-06-2001||08:52 PM||||I have a broken bolt on the front head, on the port closest to the front of the engine. After 2 days of laying under the car with a small air drill, I was finally able to get a holl deep enough to get an ez out in. When I tried to loosen the remainder of the bolt, the ez out broke off flush with the head. I could just puke. It's still like that, and I have that lovely ticking noise when I accelerate.||||reg||1
Z||000005||tgowens||04-07-2001||02:43 AM||||take the rear cradle bolts out, remove one dog bone bolt, remove the wheels, remove the deck lid. Now, tilt the engine down in the rear and it will allow access to the front manifold. now, a right angle drill and an easy out should do it - make sure you drill dead center on the bolt (use an inspection mirror if you need to to see it) or you will just enlarge the bolt hole in the block! ||||reg||1
